/Title (Digit 9 is the easiest \(F1 0.9932\) and digit 1 has the highest recall \(99.55%\). The weakest results involve digit 0, which has the lowest precision \(91.42%\), and digit 6, which has the lowest recall \(91.82%\): 16 of the 220 test utterances of digit 6 are predicted as digit 0. Digit 8 is also confused with digit 3 \(9 utterances\), and digit 3 is sometimes predicted as digit 7 \(6 utterances\).)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1688 0 R
/First 1689 0 R
/Last 1689 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 1516 0 R
>>
endobj
1630 0 obj
<<
/Title (Figure7 shows the mean gradient norm before clipping during training. The vanilla RNN has large and erratic gradient norms, with spikes of about 30 and 25 against a clipping threshold of 5, whereas the LSTM and GRU gradient norms stay below about 2 and decrease smoothly. This irregular behavior of the RNN is consistent with its noisy loss curve and with the need for gradient clipping.)
/Dest [28 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1516 0 R
/Next 1690 0 R
>>
