/Title (The maximum validation and maximum test accuracies in Table 3 aretaken independently, so they can come from different configurations \(for example,thebest LSTM test accuracy belongs to LSTM_64_lr001\). The gated cells outperform thevanilla RNN on validation accuracy by about 2.6 to 3.0 points, but the test accuraciesofthe three architectures lie within about 1.3 points of each other, so the evidence foraclear winner on this split is limited.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1852 0 R
/Prev 1866 0 R
/Next 1867 0 R
>>
endobj
1886 0 obj
<<
/Title (Figure6 displays onetest image of each digit as the sequence that the recurrent layer actually receives: each row is a time step and each column is one of the 28 pixel features. The heatmaps show that the position of the stroke shifts from row to row, so the network must integrate information across time steps to separate digits whose shapes differ only in how the strokes evolve vertically.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1867 0 R
>>
endobj
1887 0 obj
<<
/Title (The experiment shows that recurrent networks can classify Kannada digits when each image is read as a sequence of 28 rows. The 64-unit GRU was selected by validation accuracy \(91.15%\) and obtained 89.58% test accuracy, with digit 6 \(often confused with digit 7\) as the most difficult class. LSTM and GRU models trained more smoothly and reached higher validation accuracy than the vanilla RNN, although the test accuracies)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1868 0 R
>>
endobj
1888 0 obj
<<
/Title (of all three architectures are close. Since the configurations differ in several hyperparameters at once and several runs used the full 20-epoch limit, a longer training budget and a controlled one-factor-at-a-time search would be needed to separate the effects of model size, dropout and learning rate.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1868 0 R
/Next 1869 0 R
>>
endobj
